  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"There are fewer cups than peak simultaneous requests, but guests can wait or use their own cups.\", \"decision\": \"How should available cups be allocated during busy periods?\", \"goal\": \"Make cup lending and return convenient while keeping the available stock and volunteer work understandable.\", \"problem\": \"A neighborhood café wants to lend reusable cups at community gatherings and recover them afterward. It has a modest cup stock, washable collection crates, a simple lending record, and volunteers who can share short shifts. The plan concerns a small pilot with no deposit or sales system. Different decisions address separate moments in lending, return, and review.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nVolunteers ask guests when they will need a cup and allocate available cups to the earliest stated need. Allocation follows intended use time rather than request time.\nVolunteers reserve a small number of available cups for each upcoming service period and take requests within that period. Early requests cannot consume the whole afternoon’s lending stock.\nVolunteers assign available cups in request order and tell later guests when a return is expected. The waiting order is clear when stock runs short."
    }
  ],
